  "query_preview": "../Sunday through Thursday/... A low pressure system in \nthe Southern Gulf of Alaska will result in easterly flow aloft,\nwhich may lead to convective showers from Canada moving into \nSoutheast Alaska on Sunday. As of now, confidence in this \noccurring is low due to model disagreement, but it is certainly \nsomething to watch. There is greater confidence in some stronger \nwinds (up to 20 kts) from Dixon Entrance into Clarence Strait, and\nnortherly winds in Lynn Canal. By Sunday evening, the northerly \nwinds are expected to turn southerly as the pressure gradient \nflips. \n\nThe low pressure system in the Gulf of Alaska is expected to meander \nwestward from Haida Gwaii through the middle of next week. The low \npressure system looks to be fairly disorganized, but still will allow\nus to have rain chances and perhaps some stronger winds for the \nsouthern inner channels (Clarence Strait and Southern Chatham \nStrait).",
